<p class="heritage-longread__dek">A standalone family history of the Lakeman line &mdash; from the reclaimed polder soil of seventeenth-century Noord-Holland, through the spice islands of the Dutch East Indies, to a Calgary house across the street from a man-made lake, and on to three children in whom the line meets four other families.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-to-come-through"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>To come through</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">The Netherlands → the Indies → Calgary · c. 1660 to today</p><p>Every long family has a word for what carried it through. The Dutch have one. They say <em>doorkomen</em> &mdash; to come through. It is sharper than <em>survive</em>, which is passive, and than <em>endure</em>, which is merely patient. It means to break out the other side of the gale and reach the next harbour: to still be there, working, when the cholera year ends; still raising children when the empire collapses; still there at all, when by every reasonable accounting you should not be.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The Lakemans were born onto land that had been made.</p></aside><p>The Lakemans practised <em>doorkomen</em> for four hundred years. They practised it in the reclaimed soil of the Beemster polder, patching dikes and burying children. They practised it through the long decline of the Dutch Republic, the Napoleonic occupation, the cholera epidemics of the 1840s and 1850s. They practised it when a seven-year-old orphan named Pieter was placed with kin in Purmerend in 1852 because the same disease that had taken his mother three years earlier had now taken his father. They practised it when that boy grew up to board a steamer for the Dutch East Indies and built a life eight thousand miles from his village; when his son, a colonial mayor, watched the empire he served begin to end; when his grandson Rienk survived the Japanese invasion of Java in 1942, walked out with no nation to go home to, and chose instead to read the Earth for a living &mdash; and settled, at last, in Calgary, across the street from a small lake dug out of farmland.</p><p>The name itself carries a hint of all this. Its deepest probable origin is not &mdash; as later generations assumed &mdash; <em>man of the lake</em>, but <em>Lakenman</em>: cloth-man, a maker or finisher of <em>laken</em>, woven wool. Somewhere around 1600, in the Waterland villages north of Amsterdam, a Lakenman family worked cloth for a living. By the time the records become legible in the mid-1600s they had moved onto the new polder soil of the Beemster &mdash; a reclaimed lakebed drained by forty-three windmills between 1607 and 1612 &mdash; and were learning to farm instead. The Dutch say, with characteristic dryness, <em>God schiep de aarde, maar de Hollanders maakten het zelf</em> &mdash; God created the earth, but the Dutch made themselves. The Lakemans were born onto land that had been made.</p><p>This is their story, one generation at a time, from a Dutch polder in the 1660s to an Alberta household in which the Lakeman line meets four other family streams and passes, together, to three children.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-earliest-lakenmen-c-1580-1700-cloth-water-an"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>The earliest Lakenmen (c. 1580&ndash;1700) &mdash; cloth, water, and the new soil</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1580&ndash;1700 · the Waterland villages &amp; the new Beemster polder</p><p>The earliest figures in the Lakeman story exist as silhouettes &mdash; names that surface in fragmentary parish records, notarial rolls, and occupational lists, their biographies reconstructed from the patterns around them. The pre-1700 Dutch registers were uneven, and much of what was kept was later lost to flood, fire, and neglect. What survives is enough to know the shape of the family, not the men.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Broek-Waterland-canal-view.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A canal and timber houses in Broek in Waterland, north of Amsterdam" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Waterland country north of Amsterdam, where a cloth-working family called Lakenman lived before the Beemster was drained. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/sijmon-1.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A 1663 Dutch notarial deposition in secretary hand" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A 1663 Waterland notarial deposition &mdash; witnesses giving evidence over a disputed dyke. Not a Lakeman document, but the very kind of record, and world, the earliest Lakenmen lived inside.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Names that survive only as smudges in baptism books.</p></aside><p>The deepest probable ancestor is <strong>Jan Pietersz de Lakenman</strong>, a cloth merchant or dyer working in the late sixteenth century around Edam and the Purmer &mdash; small Waterland towns north of Amsterdam, in a landscape about to be utterly transformed. In 1607 an Amsterdam consortium began the most ambitious land-reclamation project then attempted: the draining of the Beemster, an inland lake of 7,200 hectares. Forty-three windmills in three tiers pumped it dry over five years; by 1612 a geometric grid of fields, dikes, and tree-lined roads was laid across soil that had never before been stood on.</p><p>By mid-century, men named <em>Lakenman</em> appear in the new polder&rsquo;s records. <strong>Pieter Jansz de Lakenman</strong>, plausibly Jan&rsquo;s son, is named in early Beemster fragments. A <strong>Sijmon Pietersz de Lakenman</strong> appears in mid-1600s Edam notarial documents as a <em>lakenwerker</em> &mdash; a cloth finisher &mdash; preserving the family trade even as the family took up farming. And by 1673 a <strong>Sijmon Lakenman</strong> is recorded in the Beemster Reformed Church register as father of a daughter, Trijntje, baptized on 4 March: <em>&ldquo;Den 4 dito, is het kind van Sijmon Lakenman gedoopt, genaemt Trijntje.&rdquo;</em> It is the first parish record to anchor the family to Beemster in nearly the form the surname carries today.</p><p>This was the world of the Dutch Golden Age &mdash; Vermeer and Rembrandt at work nearby, the VOC the most valuable corporation on Earth &mdash; but the family took no direct part in that wealth. They were polder people. They paid their tithes to the Reformed church, took their turns at <em>schouwdagen</em> &mdash; the unpaid days of dike maintenance every polder farmer owed the collective &mdash; and raised children in thatched farmhouses built on raised mounds to outlast sudden floods. Their reward, generation by generation, was the soil itself: rich, black, and theirs to work as long as they could pay the rent and patch the dike. Everything that comes later &mdash; the colonial career in Java, the geologist in Calgary &mdash; was built by men whose names survive only as smudges in baptism books. They had a foothold on land that had been a lake within their fathers&rsquo; lifetimes. That was enough.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sijmon-sijmonsz-lakeman-c-1698-c-1755-steward-of"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>Sijmon Sijmonsz Lakeman (c. 1698&ndash;c. 1755) &mdash; steward of the polder&rsquo;s cycle</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1698&ndash;c. 1755 · Beemster · the first solidly-traced Lakeman</p><p>The first Lakeman whose existence rests on a clear, datable parish entry is <strong>Sijmon Sijmonsz Lakeman</strong>, baptized in the Beemster Reformed Church around 1698. His father was almost certainly the elder Sijmon of the 1673 entry &mdash; making this Sijmon the man through whom the family&rsquo;s continuity into the modern era is first solidly traced.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/KinderdijkWindmills-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A row of Dutch polder windmills along a canal" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pumping windmills that kept the polder dry &mdash; the unspoken foundation of every Lakeman generation&rsquo;s livelihood. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><p>His life was the polder&rsquo;s life. Around 1715 he married <strong>Grietje Gijsbertsdr Snijders</strong>, a woman of similarly deep Waterland roots, and they raised six children baptized in the Beemster registers between about 1712 and 1729 &mdash; among them a second son, <strong>Jan</strong>, born 1720, who would carry the line forward. The household followed the rhythms of mixed polder farming: a small dairy herd milked two or three times a day, butter and Gouda cheese for the Purmerend markets, rotations of rye, oats, and flax in the fields between the canals. Their <em>langhuis</em> &mdash; the long brick-walled, reed-thatched single-storey house of the rural Dutch north &mdash; stood on a slight rise, warmed in part by the cattle stabled under the same roof. Sundays were for the Reformed church at Beemster, where baptisms and weddings were entered in the same register that had recorded Sijmon himself.</p><p>His lifetime straddled the late zenith and slow decline of the Golden Age. The republic that had dominated the world&rsquo;s trade was, by the early 1700s, losing ground to England; the VOC&rsquo;s monopolies were eroding. Out in the polder this barely registered &mdash; the cattle still needed milking, the dikes still needed patching, market day still came every Tuesday. Sijmon&rsquo;s contribution to the story is that he was the anchor: the first Lakeman around whom the record is solid. He died around 1755, by which time his son Jan was a grown man with a farm of his own, and the polder economy passed on as steady as he had received it.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-jan-sijmensz-lakeman-1720-1777-the-polder-farmer"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>Jan Sijmensz Lakeman (1720&ndash;1777) &mdash; the polder farmer and the Enlightenment beyond it</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1720&ndash;1777 · Beemster · the polder farmer the Enlightenment passed by</p><p><strong>Jan Sijmensz Lakeman</strong> was baptized in the Beemster Reformed Church on 3 November 1720, the second son of Sijmon Sijmonsz and Grietje Snijders. He lived his whole life within sight of the polder where he was baptized, and never had reason to leave.</p><p>In 1750 he married <strong>Guurtje Lourensdr Hoogetoorn</strong>, a Beemster woman born in October 1729 into another long-settled polder family &mdash; one known for steady service on the <em>heemraadschappen</em>, the boards that managed the dikes and canals. Together they raised six children recorded in the Beemster registers: Guurtje (1751), Willempje (1753), <strong>Zijmen &mdash; also called Sijmon</strong> (1756, who would carry the next link), Gijsbert (1764), Aagje (1769), and Cornelis (1775). Jan worked the same mixed dairy-and-grain farm his father had, in a polder economy that was, if anything, growing more sophisticated &mdash; better breeding lines, better plows, dikes rebuilt to higher standards after the 1717 Christmas Flood. The community ethic of <em>schouwdagen</em> remained the unspoken foundation of all of it: fail to patch the dike, and the sea took everything back.</p><p>What is worth pausing on is what Jan&rsquo;s life was <em>not</em>. He lived through the Enlightenment &mdash; Voltaire, Diderot, Rousseau, the first stirrings of steam power and revolution &mdash; and none of it reached him. He milked cows, patched dike, buried his parents, raised six children, and took his sons to the same church his father had taken him to. But the world that Enlightenment was building would reach his descendants: the American Revolution began two years before he died, the first crack in the imperial order that would, within a century, force his line into the migrations the rest of this story turns on. He died in November 1777, aged fifty-seven, and was buried in the Beemster church grounds. His son Sijmon, twenty-one, took over the farm.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sijmon-jansz-lakeman-1756-1836-the-napoleonic-ye"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>Sijmon Jansz Lakeman (1756&ndash;1836) &mdash; the Napoleonic years and the new surname</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1756&ndash;1836 · Beemster → Beets · the Napoleonic years &amp; the new surname</p><p><strong>Sijmon Jansz Lakeman</strong>, baptized in Beemster on 2 January 1756, lived eighty years &mdash; and those eighty years contain the most disruptive transformation in Dutch history since the Reformation. The Sijmon born in 1756 inherited a Dutch Republic that had governed itself since the sixteenth century. The Sijmon who died in 1836 was a subject of a constitutional monarchy under King William I &mdash; a country reinvented, between those two states, four times over.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Beets-engraving-Sluyter-DJ.webp' ) ); ?>" alt="An engraving of the village and church of Beets, North Holland" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Beets, where Sijmon Jansz Lakeman was buried in 1836. (Period engraving.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>It was Napoleon, of all people, who pinned the surname permanently into place.</p></aside><p>The trouble began in 1795, when French revolutionary armies crossed the frozen rivers and met almost no resistance. The old Republic was abolished and replaced by the French-aligned Batavian Republic; the VOC collapsed under its debts in 1799; in 1806 Napoleon installed his brother Louis as King of Holland; in 1810 he annexed the Netherlands outright and began conscripting Dutch men into the <em>Grande Armée</em> &mdash; many to Russia, where most died. With Napoleon&rsquo;s retreat the Dutch declared themselves free in 1813, and the Congress of Vienna created the new Kingdom of the Netherlands in 1815. Sijmon, born a citizen of the old Republic, died a subject of a monarchy that had not existed in his youth.</p><p>One administrative shock is directly visible in the family record. In 1811, by Napoleonic decree, all Dutch citizens had to register fixed family surnames. Until then the Lakemans had used the patronymic system &mdash; <em>Jan Sijmensz</em>, Jan son of Sijmon &mdash; without a settled family name. After 1811 the family formally became <strong>Lakeman</strong> on the civil register. They had been called Lakeman or Lakenman informally for generations, but it was Napoleon, of all people, who pinned the surname permanently into place.</p><p>Before any of that storm arrived, in 1779, Sijmon had married <strong>Lijsbeth Klaasdr Kramer</strong> (baptized in Warder in 1758). They raised six children, all baptized in the Beemster Reformed Church: Jan (1780), Klaas (1781), <strong>Pieter Zijmonsz</strong> (baptized 13 April 1783, who would carry the family forward), Sijmen (1784), Louweris (1786), and Simon (1787). Lijsbeth died in 1826; Sijmon remarried in 1827 to Immetje Pronk, with no further children. Through all eighty years &mdash; Republic, client state, annexation, restored monarchy &mdash; his actual life remained that of a Beemster polder farmer. The polder was a place the gales of history blew over rather than through. He died on 8 September 1836 and was buried in the parish grounds at Beets.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-pieter-sijmonsz-lakeman-1783-after-1850-the-last"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Five</span>Pieter Sijmonsz Lakeman (1783&ndash;after 1850) &mdash; the last steady generation</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1783&ndash;after 1850 · Beets / Purmerend · the last steady generation</p><p><strong>Pieter Sijmonsz Lakeman</strong> was baptized in the Reformed Church at Beets, just outside Beemster, on 23 April 1783 &mdash; one of the more solid pieces of primary documentation in the early Lakeman record. He was the third son of Sijmon Jansz and Lijsbeth Kramer.</p><p>Around 1805&ndash;1810 he married <strong>Mientje Adriaansdr Kruit</strong>, a Waterland woman of similar polder background, and they raised five children: Adriaan (c. 1806), Geertje (1808), <strong>Sijmon</strong> (c. 1819, the man whose cholera death would nearly end the line), Trijntje (c. 1822), and Claes (c. 1825). He held his modest acreage in Purmerend as a <em>pachter</em> &mdash; a tenant farmer &mdash; as most Lakemans had for generations, working the familiar cycle of spring sowing, summer haying, autumn harvest, winter dike-patrol.</p><p>What changed in his lifetime changed <em>around</em> the polder rather than on it. The Industrial Revolution reached the Netherlands in the 1820s and 1830s: steam pumps began replacing windmills, and the first Dutch railway &mdash; Amsterdam to Haarlem, 1839 &mdash; soon cut the journey from Pieter&rsquo;s butter and cheese to the city markets from days to hours. The old polder economy was, for the first time, being woven into a faster national network. Pieter lived through all of it without leaving home, dying sometime after 1850 at a substantial age. His third son, Sijmon, was the one to whom the family land and line passed &mdash; and Sijmon walked straight into the worst year the family would ever see.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sijmon-lakeman-1819-1852-the-year-the-line-nearl"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Six</span>Sijmon Lakeman (1819&ndash;1852) &mdash; the year the line nearly ended</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1819&ndash;1852 · Purmerend · the year the line nearly ended</p><p><strong>Sijmon Lakeman</strong> was born in 1819 in Purmerend to Pieter Sijmonsz and Mientje Kruit, the middle son, and inherited the working share of the family land. In 1842, aged twenty-three, he married <strong>Trijntje Tergand</strong>, a Beemster woman eighteen years older &mdash; forty-one at the time of the marriage. The likeliest explanation is the ordinary one: she was a widow, or had postponed marriage, and the age gap was the practical match available to a young polder farmer.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/trijntje.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="An 1849 civil death register entry from Purmerend" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Purmerend civil death register, 5 February 1849 &mdash; the infant Cornelis Lakeman, &ldquo;kind van Sijmon Lakeman, arbeider, en Trijntje Tergand.&rdquo; One of the children lost in the cholera years. (Translated from the Dutch.)</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/WeeskamerGoes.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A Dutch Weeskamer (civil orphan-chamber) building" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Dutch <em>Weeskamer</em> &mdash; the civil orphan board that registered the placements of seven-year-old Pieter and his surviving siblings in 1852. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The whole line now hung on a seven-year-old boy.</p></aside><p>Over the next seven years they had five children, three of whom died in infancy &mdash; the first Pieter at age one in 1845, a Trijntje at nine months in 1847, Cornelis as a newborn in 1849. Two survived: their daughter Mientje, born 1842, and a second son also named <strong>Pieter</strong> (naming a new child for a lost one was common practice), born 1845 &mdash; who would become the most consequential figure in the entire Lakeman story.</p><p>Then, in 1849, the cholera came. It was one of the great pandemic waves of the century, travelling the new shipping and railway networks out of the Ganges Delta; Purmerend lost over a quarter of its young children in the 1849 wave alone. The cause &mdash; contaminated water &mdash; was not yet understood, and the standard treatments did nothing. <strong>Trijntje Tergand died of cholera on 20 February 1849</strong>, aged forty-one; her newborn son Cornelis died around the same time. Sijmon remarried with the speed rural life demanded &mdash; seven months later, on 16 September 1849, to <strong>Antje Root</strong>, a twenty-six-year-old Purmerend woman &mdash; and their daughter Marijtje was born in July 1850. For a moment the household seemed to be rebuilding.</p><p>It did not last. In June 1852 the cholera returned, and on <strong>15 June 1852 Sijmon Lakeman died in Purmerend</strong>, aged thirty-three. The civil register is starkly bureaucratic: <em>Sijmon Lakeman, arbeider, overleden</em> &mdash; Sijmon Lakeman, labourer, deceased. He left three surviving children: Mientje, ten; Pieter, seven; Marijtje, two. The Weeskamer, the Dutch civil orphan board, registered their placements with kin; the widow Antje remarried within a few years, scattering the household further. The whole line &mdash; from the 1612 reclamation through four generations of polder farmers &mdash; now hung on a seven-year-old boy. Three of his five siblings were already dead; his father was dead; his mother was dead. He survived. The next chapter is the story of what he did with that.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-pieter-lakeman-1845-c-1900-the-orphan-who-crosse"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Seven</span>Pieter Lakeman (1845&ndash;c. 1900) &mdash; the orphan who crossed the world</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1845&ndash;c. 1900 · Purmerend → Zeeland → the Dutch East Indies</p><p><strong>Pieter Lakeman</strong>, born in Purmerend in 1845, was seven years old when both parents and three of his four siblings were dead. The Weeskamer placed him with extended Lakeman or Tergand kin around Purmerend. The next two decades are thinly documented &mdash; but by his late twenties he had pulled himself out of an orphaned childhood into a working adult life.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/bloemen-huizen-water-zierikzee-2-scaled.png' ) ); ?>" alt="Canal, flowers and houses in Zierikzee, Zeeland" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Zierikzee, the Zeeland port where Pieter Lakeman married Jacoba Leunis on 5 August 1874, before the couple sailed for the Indies. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Map-of-the-Dutch-East-Indies-and-Gorontalos-commercial-shipping-network-1872-Source.png' ) ); ?>" alt="An 1872 map of the Dutch East Indies and its shipping network" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Dutch East Indies the family sailed into &mdash; their children born across the archipelago, from Muntok to Ternate to Java. (Period map, 1872.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>He left the polder, left the country, and raised his children on the other side of the world.</p></aside><p>On 5 August 1874, in the Zeeland port of Zierikzee, Pieter married <strong>Jacoba Leunis</strong>, born in the fishing village of Renesse on 1 February 1853 &mdash; daughter of an innkeeper, Leendert Leunis, and Katharina Willemina Panser. She brought exactly what his life would need: Zeeland thrift, Reformed rectitude, and the practical resilience of a coastal family who lived their whole lives within sight of the dikes that kept the North Sea out of their bedrooms. Pieter was working as a <em>koopman</em> &mdash; a trader &mdash; in the Zeeland coastal trade. But sometime in the late 1870s the couple made the largest decision in the entire Lakeman record up to that point: they left.</p><p>They sailed east, to the Dutch East Indies &mdash; modern Indonesia, then the most profitable colonial economy in the world per square mile, drawing administrators, soldiers, teachers, and traders inward as coffee, sugar, rubber, tin, and quinine flowed out. Pieter and Jacoba were part of that inbound flow, and their children&rsquo;s birthplaces map the family across the archipelago: <strong>Leonardus</strong> born around 1877 in Muntok, on the tin island of Bangka; <strong>Catharina</strong> in March 1879 in Malang, the cool highland coffee country of east Java; <strong>Geertruda</strong> around 1880 in Ternate, in the spice-laden Moluccas; <strong>Pieter Karel Willem</strong> &mdash; the future colonial mayor and the figure of the next chapter &mdash; born 27 January 1881 during a stay in The Hague; and <strong>Jacoba Helena</strong> in June 1888 in Pasoeroean, on Java&rsquo;s north coast.</p><p>Theirs was the household of an upwardly-mobile colonial trading family &mdash; Pieter&rsquo;s mercantile career reinforced by work as a schoolteacher (<em>hulponderwijzer</em>) &mdash; set in the European enclaves: a wide veranda cooled by <em>punkah</em> fans, gardens of frangipani and bougainvillea, a table laid for <em>rijsttafel</em>, the children growing up in Dutch at home and Malay with the household staff, with French and German added at school. By the late 1890s the family had repatriated to the Netherlands for good. Pieter died sometime after 1900; the exact date is not yet confirmed. Jacoba outlived him by decades, dying in occupied Netherlands in March 1942 &mdash; just weeks after the Japanese began their invasion of the same Indies where she had spent twenty-two years of her married life. She was eighty-nine. The orphan boy from Purmerend had done what none of his ancestors ever had: he left the polder, left the country, and raised his children on the other side of the world.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-pieter-karel-willem-lakeman-1881-1966-the-admini"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Eight</span>Pieter Karel Willem Lakeman (1881&ndash;1966) &mdash; the administrator and the long ending of the empire</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1881&ndash;1966 · The Hague → Magelang &amp; Malang → the Netherlands</p><p><strong>Pieter Karel Willem Lakeman</strong> &mdash; known universally in the family as <em>P.K.W.</em> &mdash; was born in The Hague on 27 January 1881 during one of his parents&rsquo; returns to the Netherlands, and spent most of his childhood back in the Dutch East Indies, where his father was building his colonial career.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Magelang_Coat_of_Arms_1930.svg_.png' ) ); ?>" alt="The 1930 coat of arms of Magelang, central Java" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The arms of Magelang, the central-Java town where P.K.W. Lakeman was <em>burgemeester</em> &mdash; mayor &mdash; from 1929 to 1934.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Dutch_colonial_architecture_in_Malang_Java_Indonesia.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Dutch colonial-era architecture in Malang, east Java" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dutch colonial Malang, the east-Java city P.K.W. administered in the mid-1930s, as the empire he served began to end. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Besturen is voorzien &mdash; to govern is to foresee.</p></aside><p>He came of age in the most confident decades of the Dutch colonial system. The <em>Ethical Policy</em> &mdash; a turn-of-the-century reform nominally committing the state to govern the Indies for the Indonesians&rsquo; benefit &mdash; gave the work a vocabulary of moral purpose, and the <em>Binnenlands Bestuur</em>, the internal administration, was an elite, examination-trained service. P.K.W. trained for it; his motto, drawn from its bureaucratic philosophy, was <em>Besturen is voorzien</em> &mdash; to govern is to foresee. His career was a long climb: a junior posting at Maros in southern Sulawesi around 1918; the batik city of Pekalongan around 1928; from 1929 to 1934 <em>burgemeester</em> &mdash; mayor &mdash; of <strong>Magelang</strong>, a garrison town set between the volcanoes Merbabu and Sumbing in central Java; and from roughly 1933 to 1936 administrator of <strong>Malang</strong>, a wealthier East Java city with a rising Indonesian nationalist movement. He rode a horse through his cities by choice &mdash; the service prized the visible, moving administrator over the office-bound one. Residents saw their mayor.</p><p>His personal life was that of a colonial-era professional. He had married briefly, before 1909, to Lodewina Bakker, who died young, around 1908, with no surviving children. He remarried in Amsterdam in 1909 to <strong>Sophia Timmermans</strong>, daughter of a man of letters and of a long Frisian line of teachers and farmers. Sophia was the cultural counterweight to his administrative bearing: she read widely, played piano, and managed multilingual households across half a dozen Indies postings. They had three sons &mdash; <strong>Rienk</strong> (born 1918 in Soerabaja), Pieter Jr. (born 1911 in Amsterdam, later a physician), and Martin &mdash; and at least one daughter.</p><p>By 1936 P.K.W. could feel the empire ending. The nationalist movement was drawing crowds the authorities could no longer dismiss; the Depression had crushed the export economy; the Dutch state was exhausted. He retired from active administration in the mid-1930s, went home to the Netherlands, and took up the quieter role of candidate notary in Amsterdam in 1946. He did not live to see the full collapse, but it came: the Japanese invaded in March 1942, defeated the colonial army in nine days, and interned every European in the colony; the Dutch fought a failed war from 1945 to 1949 to recover the islands, and Indonesia became independent under Sukarno in 1949. By the time P.K.W. died in 1966, in his eighty-fifth year, the empire he had spent his working life administering had been gone for seventeen years. Shortly before his death he met his youngest grandson &mdash; Martin Gerard Lakeman, a child of nine &mdash; for the first and only time: an old colonial mayor holding the hand of a boy who would grow up under Calgary skies.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-rienk-lakeman-sr-1918-1993-the-geologist-who-cam"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Nine</span>Rienk Lakeman Sr. (1918&ndash;1993) &mdash; the geologist who came through the war</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1918&ndash;1993 · Soerabaja → the war → Shell → Calgary</p><p><strong>Rienk Lakeman Sr.</strong> was born in Soerabaja, on the northeast coast of Java, in 1918 &mdash; eldest son of P.K.W. and Sophia Timmermans. The world he was born into was the high noon of Dutch colonial confidence; the world he would die in, eight decades later, was a Canadian city across the street from a man-made lake.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/grandpa-lakeman-1.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Rienk Lakeman Sr. holding his infant grandson Thomas, Calgary, about 1980" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Rienk Lakeman Sr. &mdash; the geologist who came through the war &mdash; holding his grandson Thomas, the keeper of these stories. Calgary, about 1980.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/grand-marquis-lede-667dd777c385c.avif' ) ); ?>" alt="A 1970s Mercury Grand Marquis" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Mercury Grand Marquis that, by his grandsons&rsquo; accounts, &ldquo;drove like a ship.&rdquo; (Period model.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Had, in the end, found water again.</p></aside><p>His childhood was the childhood of an administrator&rsquo;s son in the late-classical phase of empire, the household moving every few years as P.K.W.&rsquo;s postings rotated through the islands &mdash; Makassar, Maros, Pekalongan, Magelang, Malang. Each town widened the boy&rsquo;s frame: Sulawesi&rsquo;s peaks and coral coasts, Java&rsquo;s terraced rice, the bigger cities&rsquo; mosques beside churches beside temples. He learned languages the way other children learned games &mdash; Dutch, German, English, Malay, Indonesian, French &mdash; until he could move easily through seven. What formed in him by his late teens was a philosopher&rsquo;s curiosity inside a scientist&rsquo;s discipline: he looked at mountains the way his ancestors had looked at dikes, not as scenery but as engineering problems with histories. He was, before anyone had the word for it, already a geologist.</p><p>Then, in March 1942, the world he had been raised in disintegrated. The Japanese army invaded; the colonial army surrendered in nine days; every European was interned, families broken across camps, rations cut to starvation through the worst years. Rienk was twenty-four. He did not speak of those years often &mdash; the silences he kept around them were the kind common to men who learned, by hard practice, not to talk about what had happened to them. He came through; many of his cousins and neighbours did not. By 1949 the war for the Indies was over, the Dutch had lost, and an entire generation of Indies-born Dutchmen had to choose what to do with the rest of their lives.</p><p>Rienk chose geology &mdash; work that, like patching a dike, took the long view and trusted patience over noise. He took his degree, joined Royal Dutch Shell, and worked oilfields across the Middle East, Venezuela&rsquo;s Maracaibo basin, and West Africa before the postwar Canadian energy boom drew him to <strong>Calgary</strong>. Somewhere along the way he married <strong>Suzanna Verboom</strong>, born in 1918 in Ter Aar, Zuid-Holland, into a polder farming and merchant family; she would live to one hundred, dying in Calgary in 2018. They had at least one son: <strong>Martin Gerard Lakeman</strong>, born 1957 in Calgary.</p><p>His Calgary house was full of the past &mdash; Persian rugs on maple floors, a hi-fi wired through the walls so the music followed him room to room, National Geographics stacked by the year, Indonesian carvings on the shelves, a Mercury Grand Marquis in the garage that, by his grandsons&rsquo; accounts, <em>drove like a ship</em>. The house faced Lake Bonavista, a small artificial lake dug from farmland in the late 1960s. The man who had crossed two oceans, survived an imperial collapse, and read the Earth for forty years had, in the end, found water again. He played the <em>1812 Overture</em> with the cannons at full volume, climbed mountains into his sixties and trees into his seventies, and spoke seven languages &mdash; but laughed loudest in the language of family. He died in Calgary in 1993, at seventy-five.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-martin-gerard-lakeman-b-1957-the-calgary-generat"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Ten</span>Martin Gerard Lakeman (b. 1957) &mdash; the Calgary generation</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">b. 1957 · Calgary · the first Lakeman born in Canada</p><p><strong>Martin Gerard Lakeman</strong> was born in Calgary in 1957 &mdash; the first Lakeman in three centuries born outside the Netherlands or the Dutch East Indies. He arrived in a city just beginning its turn from a cattle-and-railway town into the energy capital of the country; the Leduc oil strike of 1947 had set the trajectory, and the Calgary of his childhood was a town in the middle of becoming a metropolis.</p><p>His was the first Lakeman generation whose primary identity was Canadian. He grew up speaking English with a Calgary accent, going to Calgary schools and Stampedes, his father&rsquo;s Indonesian carvings and his mother&rsquo;s Dutch manners giving him a layer of European-and-Asian inheritance most of his neighbourhood lacked &mdash; but his daily life was Albertan. He carried forward what his father had built: the family&rsquo;s working relationship with the energy industry, the multilingual ear, the disciplined relationship with work that runs back to the seventeenth-century polder.</p><p>In the late 1970s he married, and in 1980 he had a son: <strong>Thomas Martin Cheesman</strong> &mdash; the boy through whom the Lakeman blood runs forward into the present. The surname Thomas carries, <em>Cheesman</em>, came not from Martin but from his step-father Brian Cheesman&rsquo;s family &mdash; a chosen name, not a blood one. The Lakeman line is the blood; the Cheesman name is the channel it was poured into. Martin&rsquo;s generation completed the family&rsquo;s long transition: the Lakemans who had crossed the world east in the 1870s became, in his era, simply Albertans &mdash; local people with deep international roots. The polder and the Indies and the war were stories his children would hear from their grandfather; the daily texture of their lives was Calgary.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-lakeman-river-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Lakeman river joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Calgary · Grande Prairie · where five rivers meet</p><p>Every long family story has a moment when it stops being history and becomes the present. For the Lakemans, that moment is now.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/grandparents-with-fam.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Three generations of the family together under a tree" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The family today &mdash; three generations in whom the Lakeman line meets four others. Grande Prairie.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Doorkomen. They came through.</p></aside><p>The Lakeman current runs out of the Beemster polder, through the Indies and a world war and two oceans, and reaches the present through one man: <strong>Thomas Martin Cheesman</strong>, born in Calgary on 4 November &mdash; Lakeman through his father, Martin Gerard Lakeman. Thomas has spent his adult life as the family&rsquo;s quiet archivist &mdash; keeper of the slides and photographs, maker of the family-tree spreadsheets, writer of the website where these stories live. He grew up across from a lake, the way his grandfather Rienk had.</p><p>But Thomas is less the end of a line than one of its confluences &mdash; and Calgary, the city he was born in, is where two of his own currents had already met. The Lakeman line reached Calgary when Rienk settled there in the 1950s, after the polder and the Indies and the war. From the other direction, his mother&rsquo;s people had been arriving in Alberta too: the <strong>Docherty</strong> line out of Ireland and industrial Lanarkshire, and with it the <strong>McIver, Campbell, and Cameron</strong> lines out of the Hebrides, by way of a Saskatchewan crofter colony. Those maternal streams came down through his mother, Elizabeth Maryanne Cheesman, born Docherty. So Thomas&rsquo;s own blood is itself a meeting of waters &mdash; his father&rsquo;s Dutch-Indies current and his mother&rsquo;s Irish-Hebridean current, running at last in one channel in the city where he was born.</p><p>And the meeting did not stop with him. Thomas and <strong>Melanie Lyn Haiste</strong> &mdash; born in Grande Prairie, married to Thomas in Calgary on 2 July 2016 &mdash; together carry their inheritances into one household. Melanie brings two currents of her own: the <strong>Haiste</strong> line through her father, Dan Steven Haiste, and the <strong>Rycroft</strong> and <strong>Steinke</strong> lines through her mother, Lana Lyn Rycroft. The Lakeman line is therefore one of five now running together &mdash; the Lakeman, Docherty, and Hebridean lines through Thomas; the Haiste and Rycroft lines through Melanie; and the Cheesman name, chosen rather than inherited, that gathers them all.</p><p>The three who inherit it are a sibling-group, and they inherit jointly:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Patience Kristie-Anne Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie on 24 September 2013</li>
<li><strong>Daniel Eric Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie on 30 June 2015</li>
<li><strong>Faith Lana Lyn Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie on 30 March 2017</li>
</ul><p>No one of them is <em>the heir</em>. Each carries the whole of it &mdash; three and a half centuries of polder labour, a colonial career in the Indies, a survived world war, a transatlantic migration, and, braided in beside the Lakeman thread, the Irish and Hebridean and Yorkshire and Peace-Country streams that met theirs.</p><p>None of the old Lakemans knew these three, or could have imagined the world they were born into. The cloth-finisher of seventeenth-century Edam could not have imagined an aircraft; the polder farmer Jan Sijmensz could not have imagined the Indies; the cholera orphan Pieter could not have imagined Calgary. What every one of them practised, generation by generation, without needing a word for it except in the Dutch &mdash; is the thing that brought the family through plague, war, empire, occupation, and the slow ending of one world after another.</p><p><em>Doorkomen.</em> They came through. Patience, Daniel, and Faith inherit it together &mdash; the word and the thing the word names. The world they will live through is not yet written. But the family has been here before, and it came through. It will again.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-lineage-at-a-glance">Lineage at a glance</h2><div class="heritage-longread__table-wrap"><table class="heritage-longread__table">
