</table></div><p><strong>Confidence tiers:</strong> <em>Verified</em> &mdash; anchored to at least one primary register (DTB parish book, civil register, colonial service or government record). <em>Partly verified</em> &mdash; one anchor point against an otherwise inferred generation. <em>Inherited</em> &mdash; rests on family genealogy and circumstantial inference without primary verification yet. <em>Living memory</em> &mdash; recent enough to be remembered directly by family.</p><div class="heritage-longread__ribbon" aria-hidden="true"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sijmon Lakenman</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">fl. 1673</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sijmon Sijmonsz</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1698&ndash;1755</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Jan Sijmensz</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1720&ndash;1777</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sijmon Jansz</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1756&ndash;1836</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Pieter Sijmonsz</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1783&ndash;after 1850</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sijmon</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1819&ndash;1852</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Pieter</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1845&ndash;c. 1900</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">P.K.W.</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1881&ndash;1966</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Rienk Sr.</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1918&ndash;1993</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Martin</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1957</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Thomas</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1980</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">2013&ndash;2017</span></span></div><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>Side-branches named in the records but off the direct line: Pieter the emigrant&rsquo;s other children, born across the Indies &mdash; <strong>Leonardus</strong> (b. c. 1877, Muntok), <strong>Geertruda</strong> (b. c. 1880, Ternate), <strong>Catharina Wilhelmina Jacoba</strong> (b. 1879, Malang; twice-widowed, d. Hilversum 1960), and <strong>Jacoba Helena</strong> (b. 1888, Pasoeroean); P.K.W.&rsquo;s first wife <strong>Lodewina Bakker</strong> (d. c. 1908) and his other sons by Sophia Timmermans &mdash; <strong>Pieter Jr.</strong> (b. 1911, a physician) and <strong>Martin</strong> &mdash; alongside the direct line through Rienk; and the deep polder households whose many children fill the Beemster DTB registers. The three children at the foot of the table inherit the Lakeman line jointly with the Docherty, McIver, Haiste, and Rycroft lines, gathered under the chosen Cheesman name.</em></p><details class="heritage-longread__notes">
<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>The Lakeman line is unusually well-anchored in primary records &mdash; far better than most European emigrant family histories &mdash; because the Dutch <em>DTB</em> registers (<em>Doop-, Trouw- en Begraafboeken</em>, the baptism, marriage, and burial books of the Reformed Church) were among the best-kept in early modern Europe, and the Beemster registers in particular survived in good shape and are now digitised and indexed across the Dutch archival platforms. For most of the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, every Lakeman birth, marriage, and death is at minimum indexed and locatable. The story therefore stands on solid foundations across the central seven or eight generations, with speculative pre-1700 origins and a few open-edge gaps at the turn of the twentieth century.</p><h3>Key records still needed</h3><p>In rough order of value:</p><ol>
<li><strong>The pre-1700 cloth-merchant generations</strong> &mdash; Jan Pietersz, Pieter Jansz, and Sijmon Pietersz de Lakenman rest on patronymic inference and notarial fragments. The earliest <em>fully</em> anchored Lakeman is the 1698-baptized Sijmon Sijmonsz; everything before him is plausible but not yet proven. Pre-civil-registration parish books at the <strong>Noord-Hollands Archief</strong> (Haarlem) are the route in.</li>
<li><strong>Pieter Lakeman&rsquo;s death date and place</strong> (after 1900) and the <strong>exact dates of P.K.W.&rsquo;s colonial postings</strong> &mdash; the colonial personnel files at the <strong>Nationaal Archief</strong> (The Hague) would close most of these gaps.</li>
<li><strong>Pieter Sijmonsz&rsquo;s marriage and death records</strong> (c. 1805&ndash;1810; after 1850) &mdash; not yet located in transcribed registers.</li>
<li><strong>City-based marriage and burial records</strong> at the <strong>Stadsarchief Amsterdam</strong> for the Hague/Amsterdam generations.</li>
</ol><h3>Notes</h3><p><strong>The verified spine.</strong> From the 1811 civil-register era onward the record is detailed: Sijmon&rsquo;s 1852 cholera death entry, Pieter and Jacoba&rsquo;s 1874 Zierikzee marriage, P.K.W.&rsquo;s 1881 Hague birth registration, and his colonial service records are all primary documents. The 1673 Beemster baptism of Trijntje, daughter of Sijmon Lakenman, is the deepest single anchor.</p><p><strong>The Indies years are unusually well-supported.</strong> Although many Indies records were lost in the Japanese occupation, enough survived in The Hague to make P.K.W.&rsquo;s administrative career one of the better-documented stretches of the whole story &mdash; supported, too, by the family&rsquo;s own photographs and papers.</p><p><strong>The Canadian generations</strong> &mdash; Rienk&rsquo;s postwar Calgary career onward &mdash; rest on Canadian census, immigration, and government records, and on living memory.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>In short: believe the polder, and check the deepest stones. The central generations are firm; the pre-1700 cloth-men and a few late-Indies dates are the seams still waiting to be closed.</em></p>
